    "content": "A new tax increase implemented under Somaliland's 2026 budget is taking a heavy toll on small business owners in Hargeisa, the region's capital. The measure, which raises the rate on business earnings, has led to the closure of numerous small shops, particularly those run by low-income residents.\n\nOne such case is Anab Jama Furre, a 92-year-old woman who operated a food and general goods store for more than ten years. The shop was the sole source of income for her household of seven. Anab reported that she used to earn $5 to $6 daily, which covered essentials like food and water. Due to the increased tax, she could not sustain the business and was forced to shut it down.\n\nWith the shop closed, Anab's family now faces severe hardship. She explained that they previously enjoyed three meals a day, but now they can only afford to cook once. Her situation reflects a broader trend in Hargeisa, where many small retailers are struggling to cope with the fiscal changes.\n\nSomaliland, which declared independence from Somalia in 1991 but has not received widespread international recognition, relies heavily on domestic taxation to fund its government. The 2026 budget introduced higher tax rates to boost revenue, but the move has disproportionately affected small-scale traders who operate on thin margins.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development is significant. Many diaspora members send remittances to relatives in Somaliland, often to help them start or sustain small businesses. With such enterprises becoming less viable, the economic lifeline for many families is at risk, and the diaspora may need to reconsider their support strategies.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Ergo here: https://radioergo.org/en/2026/09/tax-rise-in-somaliland-puts-poor-hargeisa-shop-owners-out-of-business/",
    "source": "radioergo.org",
    "source_name": "Radio Ergo",
    "original_url": "https://radioergo.org/en/2026/09/tax-rise-in-somaliland-puts-poor-hargeisa-shop-owners-out-of-business/",
